t weak players tend to bet strong hands.\n\nIn high-level play, sometimes you check strong hands, but here, with all checks, it’s more likely both are weak.\n\nAnother angle: **you have the Ace of Hearts** — but irrelevant.\n\nBut **no diamond in your hand**, so no blocker to A♦ or J♦.\n\nBut still — action suggests weakness.\n\nBest move: **check back** — take the pot down safely.\n\nBecause:\n- If you bet, only worse hands fold, better hands call or raise — classic "bad beat" scenario\n- So **no value in betting** — you won’t get called by worse very often\n\nFor example:\n- Hands worse than you: pairs (K, Q, etc.) — likely fold to a bet\n- Hands better than you: A♦, J♦ — likely call or raise\n- So betting has **negative expected value**\n\nTherefore, **checking is best** — you win against all non-flush hands.\n\nAnd since opponent checked, they likely don’t have flush.\n\nThus, **check and show down**.\n\nReason: Opponent has shown extreme passivity throughout all streets, suggesting a weak hand. The board is flush-heavy, and their river check indicates they likely do not have a strong diamond holding. Betting would only induce folds from worse hands or calls/raises from better flushes, offering poor value. Checking ensures you win the pot against bluffs or weak pairs while minimizing risk.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
